            <ArticleTitle>[Low back pain in general medical practice].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>59-61</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To characterize prevalence and features of low back pain (LBP) in Moscow outpatients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MATERIAL AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The questionnaire designed in the Rheumatology Institute of Russian Medical Academy was used in the trial performed for a month with participation of 1300 Moscow outpatients aged 18 to 50 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">LBP is a frequent cause of consulting a physician in an outpatient practice. Most of the outpatients complained of acute and subacute moderate or severe LBP. This pain often caused disability. Of diagnostic tools most frequent was x-ray investigation of the spine.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Spinal osteochondrosis is the most frequent diagnosis made in LBP outpatients. Detectability of threat marks in LBP patients is not more than 10%.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
